 r←CreateReleaseParms
⍝ Returns a namespace with every variable a GitHub release is made of, carrying defaults.\\
⍝ `CreateRelease` starts from this and fills in whatever it can collect. You may also
⍝ amend it yourself and pass it to `CreateRelease` as its left argument: what you set
⍝ then takes precedence over what would have been collected.\\
⍝ Note that a release is not a package. Nothing here describes the package, only this
⍝ one release, which is why there is no "description": what GitHub puts on display as
⍝ the text of a release is `body`, and that is where the release notes go.\\
⍝ The variables are taken as they are, so if you override `version` you are expected to
⍝ override `tag` and `releaseTitle` as well: they are not derived from it a second time.
 r←⎕NS''
 r.owner←''             ⍝ The owner of the repository on GitHub
 r.repo←''              ⍝ The name of the repository
 r.version←''           ⍝ Without any build metadata: "0.19.0", not "0.19.0+64"
 r.tag←''               ⍝ What the release points at; "gh" creates the tag if need be
 r.releaseTitle←''      ⍝ Defaulted by `CreateRelease` to "Version 1.2.3"
 r.body←⍬               ⍝ The release notes: a vector of text vectors
 r.assets←⍬             ⍝ Files to attach, relative to `projectHome` unless absolute;
                        ⍝ `CreateRelease` offers every file of the distribution folder
 r.tatinPackage←0       ⍝ 1 = consumed from tatin.dev, so no assets and a line saying so
 r.draft←1              ⍝ 1 = created but not published, so nothing goes public by accident
 r.prerelease←0         ⍝ 1 = GitHub marks it as a pre-release
 r.target←''            ⍝ Branch or commit; empty means the repository's default branch
 r.projectHome←''       ⍝ Where the project lives on disk
 r.missing←⍬            ⍝ What could not be collected; `CreateRelease` fills this
⍝Done
